1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,048
JAMIE: Tesla's robotaxi just got banned in California—the place that literally invented cars—and somehow Broadcom's up four point three-nine percent anyway.
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/transportation/962309/new-jersey-robotaxi-bill-lidar-tesla

2
00:00:07,048 --> 00:00:20,475
ALEX: The S&P dropped zero point four-three percent, Nasdaq barely held at minus zero point three, but the V I X spiked six point eight-two percent—which is the market's way of saying investors got nervous without actually selling anything yet.

3
00:00:20,475 --> 00:00:25,846
JAMIE: So we're in that weird zone where people are *thinking* about panicking but haven't committed.

4
00:00:25,846 --> 00:00:36,587
ALEX: Exactly. Tesla down two percent, Meta down one point seven-seven—the usual suspects getting punished—but Broadcom's still up four point three-nine because chip demand apparently survives even when robotaxis get banned in California.

5
00:00:36,587 --> 00:00:45,650
MAYA: Hold on, so we're saying the market's like a person who's stressed about their job but still buying premium coffee on the way out the door?

6
00:00:45,650 --> 00:00:57,398
MAYA: The thing is, California's already got a permitting framework for robotaxis. But the draft regulation coming out specifically says you need lidar or radar. Tesla doesn't have either. It's just cameras and neural nets.

7
00:00:57,398 --> 00:01:08,811
ALEX: So this isn't the state saying 'your safety data is bad.' It's the state saying 'we don't care if you think you solved it—use the hardware stack we're mandating or don't operate here.'

8
00:01:08,811 --> 00:01:14,517
JAMIE: And Elon's response to being told to follow the permitting process was basically to ignore it?

9
00:01:14,517 --> 00:01:28,615
MAYA: He's been running Cybercab rides in San Francisco for months without a permit. No regulatory approval, no public testing license, just cars on streets. Regulators noticed. So now the state's writing a rule that would make what he's already doing illegal.

10
00:01:28,615 --> 00:01:40,699
ALEX: It's regulatory response to defiance, not safety evidence. Which means even if Tesla's cameras work fine, the law can still exclude them because the mechanism isn't about capability—it's about who gets to set the rules.

11
00:01:40,699 --> 00:01:54,461
MAYA: Meta's now letting anyone make AI images from your public Instagram photos without you having to explicitly sign up for it—you just have to opt out, which is the privacy equivalent of a fire exit hidden behind a velvet rope.
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/07/meta-rolls-out-muse-a-new-ai-image-generator/

12
00:01:54,461 --> 00:01:57,818
JAMIE: So the default is 'we own your face now'?

13
00:01:57,818 --> 00:02:13,258
MAYA: Right. And Meta's defense is 'you can turn it off in settings,' but privacy advocates are saying this is a recipe for disaster because most people won't know it's happening, and by the time they find out, their likeness has already been fed into a model.

14
00:02:13,258 --> 00:02:18,629
ALEX: The Ray-Ban glasses have a privacy light on them. This feature makes that light meaningless.

15
00:02:18,629 --> 00:02:27,356
JAMIE: Next up: the IMF just said an Iran conflict would leave inflation scars through twenty twenty-seven—we're talking a multi-year oil shock, not a one-quarter thing.

16
00:02:27,356 --> 00:02:40,783
ALEX: They're forecasting that even if the immediate military shock doesn't crater growth, energy prices stay elevated long enough to keep wage pressure and input costs high through the rest of the decade. One geopolitical event, three years of inflation drag.

17
00:02:40,783 --> 00:02:50,517
MAYA: Venus Williams backed an app that locks your other apps until you hit your step count for the day. It's called WeWard, and it claims to boost walking.
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/08/venus-williams-backed-weward-can-now-lock-your-apps-until-you-hit-your-steps/

18
00:02:50,517 --> 00:02:56,559
JAMIE: So your phone becomes a hostage until your legs cooperate. That's not gamification—that's extortion with a pedometer.

19
00:02:56,559 --> 00:03:15,356
MAYA: Weight-loss jabs are now reshaping consumer spending in ways nobody predicted—people on G L P-1s are buying mouthwash and hair dye at record rates because nausea and hair loss are apparently part of the package. The B B C looked at this and found that reduced appetite isn't the only side effect changing shopping carts.

20
00:03:15,356 --> 00:03:21,398
JAMIE: So they're not just eating less—they're dealing with the biological fallout and buying their way through it?

21
00:03:21,398 --> 00:03:37,845
ALEX: It's a secondary market nobody planned for. You lose weight, you lose hair, your mouth tastes like pennies—suddenly oral care and cosmetics companies are getting a demand surge that has nothing to do with vanity and everything to do with a drug's side effects becoming a revenue stream.

22
00:03:37,845 --> 00:03:47,580
JAMIE: On the tape tomorrow: Helen of Troy reports on July eighth—the housewares company that somehow survived the pandemic and the post-pandemic, so we'll see if they're still standing.

23
00:03:47,580 --> 00:03:59,999
ALEX: Butler National and Enerpac Tool also report the same day. Butler's industrial equipment, Enerpac's hydraulic tools—both are proxies for whether manufacturing demand is still real or just a sugar high from the infrastructure bill wearing off.

24
00:03:59,999 --> 00:04:12,083
MAYA: And Sono Tek's on the same slate—they make ultrasonic coating systems. Tiny company, but if you want to know whether precision manufacturing is still hiring or if we're finally hitting the wall, that's your canary.

25
00:04:12,083 --> 00:04:23,160
MAYA: Elon's strategy is basically 'prove I'm right by operating illegally until the state has to write a law to stop me'—which is less disruption and more just speeding up the regulatory clock.

26
00:04:23,160 --> 00:04:29,873
ALEX: Except the clock now has his company's exclusion built into it before the robotaxi ever leaves the parking lot.

27
00:04:29,873 --> 00:04:34,573
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
